## Method
All steps run in one Python script, `scripts/sample_pca.py`, in the `gars-bio` environment
(via `_system/gars-env.sh`), submitted as a single batch job. Inputs are read only; nothing is
written outside this analysis directory.

1. Resolve both inputs at run time with
   `_system/resolve_artifact.py --project projects/run-99012efd --assay rnaseq_bulk --consumes counts_gene design`;
   abort if resolution fails.
2. Load the count matrix with pandas: index `gene_id`, drop the `gene_name` column, keep the
   sample columns. Load the design and check that its `sample_id` set equals the matrix's
   sample columns exactly; abort on any mismatch (no silent dropping of samples).
3. Round the length-scaled counts to integers (DESeq2 requires integer counts; this is the
   standard treatment of salmon `gene_counts_length_scaled`). Drop genes with zero counts in
   every sample.
4. Variance-stabilising transform with pydeseq2 0.5.4: build a `DeseqDataSet` with design
   `~condition`, estimate size factors, and apply `vst(use_design=False)` (blind, as is
   standard for exploratory QC so the transform is not informed by the labels being plotted).
5. Select the 500 genes with the highest variance across samples in the VST matrix (all
   retained genes if fewer than 500 remain — the matrix currently has 200 genes, so in practice
   all of them).
6. PCA with scikit-learn 1.9.0 `PCA` on the selected genes, samples as observations, genes
   centred (not scaled), `n_components = min(n_samples, n_genes)` (6 here). Record each
   component's explained-variance ratio.
7. Plot PC1 vs PC2 with matplotlib 3.11.1: one point per sample, colour by `condition`
   (two categorical colours with a legend), each point labelled with its `sample_id`, axis
   labels giving the percentage of variance explained. Save as PNG (300 dpi) and PDF.
8. Write the per-sample PC scores (PC1-PC6, joined with `condition`, `group`, `replicate`) and
   the per-component explained variance as CSV tables.

